    <!-- http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#ChatroomClient -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#ChatroomClient">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#CollaborativeSoftware"/>
        <definition>Client software used to describe conduct any form of synchronous conferencing, occasionally even asynchronous conferencing. The term can thus mean any technology ranging from real-time online chat and online interaction with strangers (e.g., online forums) to fully immersive graphical social environments.</definition>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Chat_room"/>
        <rdfs:label>Chatroom Client</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel>Chat Room Client</skos:altLabel>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#ChildProcess -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#ChildProcess">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#Process"/>
        <definition>A child process in computing is a process created by another process (the parent process). This technique pertains to multitasking operating systems, and is sometimes called a subprocess or traditionally a subtask. There are two major procedures for creating a child process: the fork system call (preferred in Unix-like systems and the POSIX standard) and the spawn (preferred in the modern (NT) kernel of Microsoft Windows, as well as in some historical operating systems).</definition>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Child_process"/>
        <rdfs:label>Child Process</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Parent_process"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


