            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1543.004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1543.004">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1543"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#modifies"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#PropertyListFile"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1543.005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1543.005">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1543"/>
        <attack-id>T1543.005</attack-id>
        <definition>Adversaries may create or modify container or container cluster management tools that run as daemons, agents, or services on individual hosts. These include software for creating and managing individual containers, such as Docker and Podman, as well as container cluster node-level agents such as kubelet. By modifying these services, an adversary may be able to achieve persistence or escalate their privileges on a host.</definition>
        <rdfs:label>Container Service</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1546 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#T1546">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#PersistenceTechnique"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#PrivilegeEscalationTechnique"/>
        <attack-id>T1546</attack-id>
        <definition>Adversaries may establish persistence and/or elevate privileges using system mechanisms that trigger execution based on specific events. Various operating systems have means to monitor and subscribe to events such as logons or other user activity such as running specific applications/binaries. Cloud environments may also support various functions and services that monitor and can be invoked in response to specific cloud events.(Citation: Backdooring an AWS account)(Citation: Varonis Power Automate Data Exfiltration)(Citation: Microsoft DART Case Report 001)</definition>
        <rdfs:label>Event Triggered Execution</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


